e valid and proceed.\n    # Initialize allocations\n    alloc = [s['minimum'] for s in segments]\n    remaining = budget - total_min\n    # While remaining > 0, allocate one token to best segment\n    # Need to track current allocation per index\n    # Compute importance/next_position for each segment that hasn't reached capacity\n    n = len(segments)\n    # We can iterate remaining times, each time pick best.\n    # For efficiency, we could use heap, but with small n it's fine.\n    for _ in range(remaining):\n        best_idx = -1\n        best_score = -1\n        for i in range(n):\n            if alloc[i] < segments[i]['capacity']:\n                # next allocated position = alloc[i] + 1\n                score = segments[i]['importance'] / (alloc[i] + 1)\n                if score > best_score:\n                    best_score = score\n                    best_idx = i\n                elif score == best_score and best_idx == -1:\n                    # tie break by original order: first encountered has higher priority? Actually \"Break equal priorities by original segment order.\" Means earlier index wins.\n                    # But we need to ensure that when scores equal, we keep the earlier index.\n                    # In loop, we compare to best_score. If equal and current index < best_idx? But we need to choose earliest index. So we can: if score > best_score: update. elif score == best_score and i < best_idx: update? But best_idx is set to first encountered with that score. Since we iterate in order, the first one gets recorded. Then later if equal, we should not replace. So condition: if score > best_score: update. Else if score == best_score: do nothing (first wins). That works because we iterate in order. So we keep that.\n                    # So no else needed.\n                    pass\n        if best_idx == -1:\n            # should not happen if total cap sufficient\n            break\n        alloc[best_idx] += 1\n    # Ensure total equals budget